    "claim": "The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms not as an inductive inference that the theory is more probable, but as a non-inductive, methodological rule to maximize the potential for falsification (learning), accepting the cost that we can never rationally justify the belief that the preferred theory will succeed in the future.",
    "mechanism": "Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven and improbable) and the methodological decision of which theory to test next. Preferring the highly corroborated theory is a strategic choice because it has withstood severe tests, meaning it has high empirical content. By choosing it, we subject ourselves to the highest risk of refutation. If it fails, we learn more; if it survives, our knowledge grows. This avoids induction because we do not claim the past success predicts future success (probability); we only claim that testing the 'boldest' conjecture currently available is the most efficient engine for error elimination.",
    "counterconditions": [
